<h2>Why Landscape Lighting Matters</h2>
<p>Outdoor lighting is about far more than making things visible. The best systems create an experience, and they earn their keep in four ways.</p>

<h3>Enhances Curb Appeal After Dark</h3>
<p>A beautifully lit home naturally draws the eye. Strategically placed fixtures can highlight:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Architectural details such as columns, gables and rooflines</li>
  <li>Stonework and masonry, from Austin stone to brick</li>
  <li>Entryways and front doors</li>
  <li>Landscaping features and planting beds</li>
  <li>Mature trees like live oaks, crape myrtles and palms</li>
  <li>Decorative gardens, walls and water features</li>
</ul>
<p>Instead of appearing flat and dark, your property gains depth, texture and visual interest. Think of the exterior as a stage: during the day the sun is the spotlight; after sunset, landscape lighting takes over, and you choose what the audience sees.</p>

<h3>Increases Safety</h3>
<p>Safety is one of the most practical reasons to invest. Poorly lit walks, driveways and stairs create hazards, and even familiar spaces become hard to navigate without good visibility. Areas that commonly benefit from light include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Walkways and garden paths</li>
  <li>Front entrances and porches</li>
  <li>Pool decks</li>
  <li>Outdoor steps and changes in grade</li>
  <li>Retaining walls and raised beds</li>
</ul>
<p>A properly designed system guides visitors naturally and reduces the chance of trips and falls, without blinding anyone along the way.</p>

<h3>Improves Security</h3>
<p>Dark corners and hidden areas around a home create real concerns. Lighting alone does not prevent crime, but it significantly improves visibility around entry points, garages, side yards, gates and backyard access. Many homeowners find that a well-lit property simply feels more secure, and even, glare-free light helps security cameras capture clearer footage than a single harsh floodlight does.</p>

<h3>Extends Outdoor Living</h3>
<p>In much of Texas, the most comfortable time to be outside from May to October is after the sun goes down. Patios, outdoor kitchens, pergolas and fire pit areas become usable long after sunset when they are lit correctly. Instead of ending gatherings when darkness arrives, you can entertain well into the evening.</p>

<h2>The Main Types of Landscape Lighting</h2>
<p>Understanding fixture types helps you make smarter design decisions. Each category serves a different purpose, and most good designs combine several.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Fixture type</th><th>Best for</th><th>Typical LED output</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
