  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h3>Path Lighting</h3>
<p>Path lights are the most recognizable landscape fixtures. Installed along walkways and garden paths, they increase safety, improve navigation, define walking routes and add visual appeal. The key is restraint: too many fixtures in a straight line create a runway effect that feels artificial. Staggering lights on alternating sides, roughly 8 to 10 feet apart, produces soft overlapping pools of light instead.</p>

<h3>Uplighting</h3>
<p>Uplighting remains one of the most dramatic techniques in outdoor design. Fixtures sit near the base of an object and aim upward. Common applications include large oak trees, palms, architectural columns, stone facades and entry features. Done well, uplighting creates focal points that draw attention from across the property. Our <a href="/services/architectural-uplighting/">architectural uplighting</a> service is built around it.</p>

<h3>Downlighting</h3>
<p>Downlighting produces a softer, more natural appearance. Fixtures are mounted above the target and shine down, often mimicking moonlight filtering through branches. It works beautifully over patios, seating areas, driveways, walkways and gardens, and many designers favor it because it feels subtle while providing excellent visibility.</p>

<h3>Spotlighting</h3>
<p>Spotlights direct focused beams toward specific features: sculptures, ornamental trees, water features, unique plantings and architectural accents. A single well-aimed spotlight with the right beam angle can turn an overlooked feature into a nighttime centerpiece.</p>

<h3>Flood Lighting</h3>
<p>Floodlights provide broader coverage for security, large lawns, driveways and property boundaries. Modern LED floods deliver impressive light with very little energy, but they need shielding and careful aiming. Our <a href="/services/security-lighting/">security and floodlighting</a> work focuses on visibility without the parking-lot glare.</p>

<h3>Step and Deck Lighting</h3>
<p>Small fixtures built into steps, decks and retaining walls provide visual guidance, reduce tripping hazards and add elegant ambiance. Even a modest deck feels more upscale when lit properly.</p>

<h2>Key Areas of Your Property to Illuminate</h2>
<p>Not every square foot needs light. Focus on the areas that deliver the greatest visual and functional impact.</p>

<h3>Front Entrance</h3>
<p>Your front door creates the first impression. Entry lighting should feel welcoming without overpowering. Consider lighting the doorway, porch columns, entry plantings and the house numbers, which also helps guests and first responders find you.</p>

<h3>Driveways</h3>
<p>Driveways occupy a lot of visual space, and on larger Texas lots they can run hundreds of feet. Lighting defines their edges and makes arrivals and departures safer. Options include path lights, bollards, downlighting from trees and accent lighting on nearby plantings. See <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway and driveway lighting</a> for layouts.</p>

