<h2>Popular Landscape Lighting Techniques</h2>
<p>The difference between average and exceptional results often comes down to technique. These are the tools professional designers reach for most.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Fixture placement</th><th>Effect</th><th>Typical beam angle</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Uplighting</td><td>At the base, aimed up</td><td>Height and drama</td><td>15°–60°</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Moonlighting</td><td>High in a tree, aimed down</td><td>Dappled, natural shadows</td><td>40°–60°</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Grazing</td><td>Close to a wall, aimed along it</td><td>Reveals stone and brick texture</td><td>Narrow to medium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Washing</td><td>Set back from a wall</td><td>Smooth, even glow</td><td>Wide (60°+)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Silhouetting</td><td>Behind the object, lighting a wall</td><td>Dark outline against light</td><td>Wide</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shadowing</td><td>In front, aimed at the object</td><td>Shadows cast on a wall</td><td>Narrow to medium</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cross lighting</td><td>Two or more angles</td><td>Balanced, three-dimensional</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h3>Moonlighting</h3>
<p>Moonlighting places fixtures high in trees and directs light downward. The effect closely resembles natural moonlight: soft shadows, a natural appearance, broad coverage and elegant ambiance. Texas live oaks, with their wide, low canopies, are ideal hosts.</p>

<h3>Silhouetting</h3>
<p>Silhouetting positions a light behind an object so it appears dark against a lit surface. It works especially well with ornamental trees, sculptures and architectural plants like agaves and sotol.</p>

<h3>Shadowing</h3>
<p>Shadowing places a fixture in front of an object to project its shadow onto a nearby surface. Trees with intricate branching, such as crape myrtles in winter, create beautiful patterns on a wall.</p>

<h3>Grazing</h3>
<p>Grazing highlights texture. Fixtures sit close to the surface, a few inches to a foot away, so even subtle relief becomes prominent. It is the signature technique for limestone, Austin stone and brick facades.</p>

<h3>Cross Lighting</h3>
<p>Cross lighting uses multiple fixtures from different angles to reduce harsh shadows and create a balanced, sculptural appearance. Professionals use it for large trees and major focal points that are seen from more than one direction.</p>

