<h2>LED Landscape Lighting: The Modern Standard</h2>
<p>Technology has transformed outdoor lighting over the past decade, and LED fixtures now dominate for good reason.</p>

<h3>Energy Efficiency</h3>
<p>LED fixtures use roughly <strong>75–80% less energy</strong> than halogen. A 4-watt LED path light replaces a 20-watt halogen lamp with similar visible output. For homeowners that means lower utility bills, reduced consumption and less environmental impact. A typical whole-property LED system costs about $10–$25 a month to run. Compare scenarios with the <a href="/tools/energy-savings-calculator/">LED energy savings calculator</a> or our article on <a href="/led-vs-halogen-landscape-lighting/">LED vs halogen</a>.</p>

<h3>Exceptional Lifespan</h3>
<p>Quality LED fixtures deliver tens of thousands of hours of operation before replacement is needed, which reduces maintenance, replacement costs and downtime. LEDs also run far cooler than halogen, which matters when ground temperatures in a Texas summer already push fixtures hard.</p>

<h3>Improved Light Quality</h3>
<p>Early LEDs sometimes produced harsh, bluish light. Modern fixtures offer warm color temperatures, accurate color rendering (look for a CRI of 90 or higher), smooth beams and consistent performance, so landscapes look natural rather than clinical.</p>

<h3>How Low-Voltage Systems Work</h3>
<p>Nearly all residential landscape lighting is a 12-volt system. A transformer plugs into an outdoor GFCI outlet and steps 120V household power down to low voltage. Direct-burial cable, usually 12- or 10-gauge, carries power to the fixtures. Over long runs, voltage drops, so a fixture 150 feet away receives less than one near the transformer. Professionals use multi-tap transformers (with 12V, 13V, 14V and 15V outputs) and hub wiring, where several fixtures connect at a single central point, to keep every light at the right voltage. Our <a href="/low-voltage-landscape-lighting-guide/">low-voltage lighting guide</a> explains it in detail.</p>

